  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nThe remnants of Hurricane Isaias over the SE CONUS will continue to \nphase with a meandering upper low over the Southern Plains and Lower \nMississippi Valley today, with widespread precipitation associated \nwith these features remaining well east of the forecast area. \n\nChances for off-and-on rain/thunderstorms across the forecast area \nenter back into the forecast by Tuesday or Tuesday night and persist \nthrough week's end, as a cold front approaches from the northwest \nand a deep upper trough approaches from the west. Increasing shear \ncoupled with modest instability and strong forcing may support a \nhandful of strong to severe thunderstorms during this time period. \nAdditionally, well above average precipitable waters may support \nheavy rainfall. \n\nThe latest model guidance has diverged from their recent consensus, \nwith the GFS and Canadian models much more progressive with this \nsystem, clearing precipitation by Thursday afternoon. In contrast, \nthe much slower ECMWF lingers precipitation chances possibly as late \nas Saturday. Stay tuned as we refine forecast details in the coming \ndays.\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nAbove average heights/thickness will support continued above average \ntemperatures through about mid next week. Daytime temperatures a few \ndegrees either side of 90 degrees look probable, especially through \nMonday or Tuesday. Additionally, stout south to southwest winds \nshould gust upwards of 30-35 mph through Monday, owing to deep and \npersistent low pressure to the west-northwest of the region. \nIncreased sustained speeds a bit from NBM today through Monday.\n\nAs we head into Thursday or Friday of next week, model consensus \nsupports a potentially drastic cool down in wake of the \naforementioned storm system, as stout Canadian high pressure settles \nsoutheast into Mid-America. The latest GFS and Canadian models \nsupport a deeper central to eastern CONUS upper trough and \nassociated cooler temperatures compared to the somewhat warmer \nECMWF. For now, ran with daytime highs mostly in the 60s-70s Friday \nand Saturday with lows in the 40s-50s, although this period could \nturn out to be cooler than currently forecast (e.g. GFS has highs in \nthe 40s Saturday!)...stay tuned.",
